<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piscataqua River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Piscataqua River is a short tidal river forming part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> and Maine, flowing into the Atlantic at Portsmouth. English fishing settlements were founded along it from 1623, among the earliest in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. Its deep harbour made Portsmouth and Kittery centres of trade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> export and shipbuilding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piscataqua-river.jpg" alt="Piscataqua River" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Decumanus at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piscataqua_River_at_Portsmouth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
